e Panel \u2013 Child & Family Services","time":"4.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Scrutiny Performance Panel - Climate Change and Nature","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-11","committee":"Governance & Audit Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-17","committee":"Scrutiny Programme Committee","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-18","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-19","committee":"Scrutiny Performance Panel \u2013 Education","time":"4.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-02","committee":"Governance & Audit Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-03","committee":"Council","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-03","committee":"Council Constitution","time":"","status":"Confirmed; Re-published 14/09/2026","webcast":false,"url":"https://swansea.moderngov.co.uk/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=520&MId=12427"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Scrutiny Programme Committee","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Scrutiny Performance Panel - Service Improvement, Regeneration and Finance","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Pension Fund Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Scrutiny Performance Panel \u2013 Adult Services","time":"4.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Scrutiny Inquiry Panel - Cost of Living","time":"10.30 am","status":"Provisional","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Scrutiny Performance Panel \u2013 Education","time":"4.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.swansea.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Swansea Council covers \u00a313.7m deficit as reserves hit minimum level","lead":"Swansea Council is forecasting it will overspend by \u00a313.678m on its day-to-day running costs for 2026/27 and plans to cover the shortfall by drawing down reserves, money councils keep aside for emergencies. The chief finance officer has warned that the council's reserves are already below the minimum level he considers acceptable before the year's overspending emerges. The forecast shortfall is driven primarily by a 15% surge in adult social care demand, with residential placements, domiciliary care and supported living packages all overspending significantly. Additional pressures include pay awards of 3.3% against the budgeted 2.5%, costing \u00a33.5m, and declining council tax collection. The council's savings programme is delivering only 97% of planned cuts, with \u00a33.835m of the savings depending on temporary solutions rather than permanent measures.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"08 - 1st Qtr Rev Budget Monitoring","paper_url":"https://swansea.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s129989/129989.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Council","paper_title":"11 - Review of Revenue Reserves 2026","paper_url":"https://swansea.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s137874/137874.pdf","headline":"Section 151 Officer reports all directorates likely to find it \"increasingly hard to live within budgets\" due to inflation and social care demand","detail":"The Section 151 Officer states it is 'likely that all Directorates will find it increasingly hard to live within budgets primarily due to both inflationary and demand pressures on services, predominantly in social care.' This describes current dynamics, not future risk, indicating budget management pressures are acute in 2026/27 and may worsen.","category":"demand","matters":"If all directorates are currently struggling with budgets, there is significant risk of in-year overspend and uncontrolled use of reserves in the current financial year. This suggests the medium-term deficit forecast may be optimistic about the council's ability to hold spending.","figures":"None specific","quote":"Equally, it is likely that all Directorates will find it increasingly hard to live within budgets primarily due to both inflationary and demand pressures on services, predominantly in social care.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Council","paper_title":"11 - Review of Revenue Reserves 2026","paper_url":"https://swansea.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s137874/137874.pdf","headline":"Council acknowledges need for major structural service change with significant costs and employment impact, but transformation plan and financing remain uncertain.","detail":"The Section 151 Officer reports that the council must 'prepare for significant change in service delivery that will inevitably impact on direct employment levels going forward' and identifies this as 'a significant financial risk given the scale and pace of budget pressures'. The officer notes transformation will require 'substantial up-front costs' and investment in digital technology, but the detailed plan and change costs are still being worked through.","category":"savings","matters":"Structural change is the mechanism for delivering the \u00a354.8m+ of required savings, but the council has not yet finalised its transformation plan or change costs, adding execution risk to the deficit recovery strategy.","figures":"None specific","quote":"It is the opinion of the Section 151 Officer that the Council needs to continue to prepare for significant change in service delivery that will inevitably impact on direct employment levels going forward.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Council","paper_title":"11 - Review of Revenue Reserves 2026","paper_url":"https://swansea.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s137874/137874.pdf","headline":"Medium-term financial plan forecasts \u00a354.8m cumulative deficit by 2029/30, with the council expecting this will worsen due to equal pay costs.","detail":"The MTFP approved in March 2026 forecast a cumulative deficit of \u00a354.8m by end of 2029/30, based on assumption of 2% annual government funding growth. Savings targets are \u00a312.7m for 2026/27 and \u00a38.3m for 2027/28. However, the Section 151 Officer warns that equal pay costs will push the deficit larger, because the council will need to both repay reserves it has drawn down and any alternative sources of financing will be costly and reduce resources elsewhere.","category":"savings","matters":"The published deficit forecast is material and conservative (assumes continued government funding growth). The officer's warning that equal pay will worsen the position suggests the council's current financial plan is incomplete and the deficit recovery task will be larger than the headline figure.","figures":"\u00a354.8m cumulative deficit by 2029/30; savings requirement \u00a312.7m (2026/27) plus \u00a38.3m (2027/28)","quote":"The Medium-Term Financial Plan approved by Council on 5th March 2026 forecast a cumulative deficit on General Fund Revenue Expenditure of some \u00a354.8m by 2029/30","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Council","paper_title":"11 - Review of Revenue Reserves 2026","paper_url":"https://swansea.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s137874/137874.pdf","headline":"\u00a322.3m of earmarked reserves are being reclassified to fund equal pay, but only \u00a34.1m is permanent; \u00a318.8m must be rebuilt.","detail":"The council is reducing earmarked reserves from \u00a382.8m to \u00a360.5m through reclassification. Of the \u00a322.3m reduction, approximately \u00a34.1m is considered permanently available for equal pay, while \u00a318.8m consists of maintenance and equipment renewal reserves that must be replenished over time. The Section 151 Officer explicitly identifies the risk: if renewal needs materialise before reserves are rebuilt, the authority may be forced to use expensive external borrowing to replace worn-out assets.","category":"reserves","matters":"The reserve draw is temporary rather than structural, betting that spending can be deferred while reserves are rebuilt. The council's own officer has flagged that this plan is risky; if renewal needs materialise before reserves are rebuilt, the authority faces unplanned borrowing costs.","figures":"Earmarked reserves reducing from \u00a382,801k to \u00a360,487k (reduction of \u00a322,314k); of reclassified reserves \u00a34.1m permanent, \u00a318.8m temporary","quote":"The overriding risk will be that the replacement of the reserve won't be achieved before the purpose of the reserve becomes an imperative expenditure item.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-10-01","committee":"Council","paper_title":"11 - Review of Revenue Reserves 2026","paper_url":"https://swansea.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s137874/137874.pdf","headline":"General reserve of \u00a310m is now below the council's certified minimum level for financial sustainability.","detail":"The general reserve was budgeted at \u00a314m in March 2026. Following the \u00a34m transfer to equal pay provisions in July, it now stands at \u00a310m. The council has formally stated this is below the minimum level it certified as adequate for an authority of its size. The Section 151 Officer notes the council is 'holding a very low level of General Reserve for the size of the authority'.","category":"reserves","matters":"General reserve is the council's contingency for unexpected events and budget smoothing. Being below the certified minimum level directly indicates reduced financial resilience and capacity to absorb further shocks.","figures":"General Fund Balance \u00a310,000k; reduced from \u00a314m budgeted in March 2026","quote":"It is now temporarily below the certified minimum level as set out in budget setting for 2026/27.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"Swansea","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-28","since_iso":"2026-08-29","distress":true,"headline":"The education capital programme has a \u00a373.571m adverse variance pending a Welsh Government cost standard review","lead":"A \u00a373.571m education capital programme variance that will force reprioritisation signals that the council's school building ambitions cannot be afforded at current cost levels, and delivery will need to be scaled back.","also":["41 other findings in this window","38 papers caught"],"watch":"Scrutiny Performance Panel \u2013 Child & Family Services on 2026-09-29","n_findings":42,"n_documents":38,"top_paper_title":"08 - 1st Qtr Rev Budget Monitoring","top_paper_url":"https://swansea.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s129989/129989.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":132,"news_drafts_total":1}}